  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 313 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe weekend will start off with a day of transition, as the stubborn \nmid-level ridge that has been over the region starts getting kicked \nwestward by a deep trough over the Eastern US seaboard. Despite the \nwaning influence of the ridge, temperatures will still rise to the \nmid 90s, with heat index values in the low 100s. Enough instability \nshould develop and beat out the subsidence of the ridge to support \nisolated to scattered afternoon showers and storms across the area. \nIts still very low confidence at this point, but with strengthening \nnortherly flow over the area, there is potential for a line of \nstrong to severe storms to drop in from the north during the late \nevening or overnight hours.\n\nRain and thunderstorm chances increase even more on Sunday, as a \nfrontal boundary pushes into the area out of the Carolinas, running \ninto the warm and moist airmass (PWATs around 2\") in place over \nGeorgia. Depending on the timing of this frontal passage, we could \nsee a threat for strong to severe storms. Temperatures will likely \nreach into the low to mid 90s, with heat index values in the upper \n90s to low 100s. Dry air will start to filter in behind the front on \nMonday, but lingering moisture could support isolated to scattered \nafternoon showers and storms. With the drier and cooler air, there'd \nbe a bit of relief from the heat, with highs likely topping out in \nthe mid to upper 80s. Unfortunately, this relief looks short-lived, \nas model guidance is in decent agreement on the ridge building back \nacross the Southeast by the middle of next week, with highs \nreturning into the 90s, and generally low rain chances.",
